        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "path": "internal/actor/agent_actor.go",
          "lineNumber": 865,
          "lineContent": "\t\toutput, err := a.agentEngine.Run(execCtx, engineInput)",
          "title": "Engine execution",
          "description": "Invokes AgentEngine.Run with messages, tools, and system prompt"
        },
        {
          "id": "2e",
          "path": "internal/actor/agent_actor.go",
          "lineNumber": 901,
          "lineContent": "\ta.dispatchHook(ctx, hooks.EventLLMResponse, map[string]any{",
          "title": "Post-execution hook",
          "description": "Dispatches LLMResponse hook with assistant text"
        },
        {
          "id": "2f",
          "path": "internal/actor/agent_actor.go",
          "lineNumber": 934,
          "lineContent": "\treplyData := agentdomain.ConsoleReplyData{",
          "title": "Reply construction",
          "description": "Builds console.reply message with response and metrics"
        },
        {
          "id": "2g",
          "path": "internal/actor/agent_actor.go",
          "lineNumber": 948,
          "lineContent": "\treturn \u0026Message{",
          "title": "Reply message",
          "description": "Returns reply message to be sent back to caller"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "AgentActor Console Message Handling\n├── handleConsoleAsk() entry \u003c-- 2a\n│   ├── Parse console.ask message \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:776\n│   ├── dispatchHook(MessageReceived) \u003c-- 2b\n│   │   └── Hook can block/observe message \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:800\n│   ├── Build prompt with memory context \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:826\n│   ├── Create cancellable execution context \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:809\n│   ├── dispatchHook(LLMRequest) \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:840\n│   ├── Engine selection branch \u003c-- 2c\n│   │   ├── if engineType == \"llmchat\"\n│   │   │   ├── Build EngineInput \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:856\n│   │   │   │   ├── Messages \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:857\n│   │   │   │   ├── getEngineTools() \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:858\n│   │   │   │   └── SystemPrompt \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:859\n│   │   │   └── agentEngine.Run() \u003c-- 2d\n│   │   │       └── [LLMChatEngine execution]\n│   │   └── else (dspy) \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:876\n│   │       └── dspyAgent.Execute() \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:877\n│   ├── dispatchHook(LLMResponse) \u003c-- 2e\n│   ├── Record turn in memory \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:915\n│   ├── dispatchHook(PostAgentTurn) \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:922\n│   ├── Build ConsoleReplyData \u003c-- 2f\n│   │   ├── Response text \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:936\n│   │   ├── Status (ok/error/cancelled) \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:937\n│   │   └── Metrics \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:938\n│   └── Return Message \u003c-- 2g\n│       └── Subject: \"console.reply\" \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:950",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nAgentActor is a **reactive actor** that wraps LLM-powered agents to handle console requests from users (via TUI or API). When a user submits a prompt through the console, the system needs to:\n\n1. **Route the message** to the appropriate agent actor\n2. **Select the right execution engine** (LLMChat or DSPy)\n3. **Execute the LLM turn** with tool-calling capabilities\n4. **Integrate hooks** for observability and control at key lifecycle points\n5. **Return a structured reply** with the assistant's response\n\nThe actor pattern transforms what could be a blocking request-response into an **event-driven message handler** that can be supervised, cancelled, and monitored.\n\n## Details\n\n### Message Reception and Validation\n\nThe `handleConsoleAsk` method [2a] is the entry point when a `console.ask` message arrives. It immediately **dispatches a MessageReceived hook** [2b] which allows external observers to block, log, or modify the incoming request before any processing begins.\n\n### Context Preparation\n\nThe actor builds the execution context by:\n- **Retrieving memory context** from short-term memory to maintain conversation continuity\n- **Creating a cancellable context** with timeout to prevent runaway executions\n- **Dispatching an LLMRequest hook** for pre-execution observability\n\n### Engine Selection and Execution\n\nThe critical routing decision happens at [2c]: the actor checks `engineType` to determine whether to use:\n\n- **LLMChatEngine**: OpenAI-compatible API with explicit tool-calling loop control\n- **DSPy agent**: dspy-go ReActAgent with its own internal loop\n\nFor LLMChatEngine, the actor constructs an `EngineInput` with messages, tools (via `getEngineTools()`), and system prompt, then invokes `agentEngine.Run()` [2d]. This triggers the full tool-calling loop described in trace 1.\n\n### Post-Execution Processing\n\nAfter the engine completes:\n1. **LLMResponse hook** [2e] fires with the assistant's text\n2. The turn is **recorded in short-term memory** for future context\n3. **PostAgentTurn hook** fires, allowing hooks to mutate the final response text\n\n### Reply Construction\n\nThe actor builds a `ConsoleReplyData` structure [2f] containing:\n- The assistant's response text\n- Status indicator (ok/error/cancelled)\n- Execution metrics (duration, token counts)\n\nThis is wrapped in an envelope and returned as a `Message` [2g] with subject `\"console.reply\"`, which the supervisor routes back to the caller.\n\n### Hook Integration Points\n\nThe system dispatches hooks at **five canonical events** during this flow:\n- MessageReceived: Can block malicious requests\n- LLMRequest: Can observe/log prompts before LLM call\n- LLMResponse: Can capture assistant responses\n- PostAgentTurn: Can mutate final response text\n- (Plus PreToolUse/PostToolUse during engine execution)\n\nThis hook architecture enables **policy enforcement, logging, and dynamic behavior modification** without coupling the core execution logic to specific observability tools."
    },
    {
      "id": "3",
      "title": "ToolRunner: Hook-Integrated Tool Execution",
      "description": "Agent engine system - tool execution with pre/post hooks for observability and input/output mutation",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "3a",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 76,
          "lineContent": "func (r *ToolRunner) Execute(ctx context.Context, call ToolCall) (ToolResult, error) {",
          "title": "Tool execution entry",
          "description": "Called by LLMChatEngine for each tool call"
        },
        {
          "id": "3b",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 80,
          "lineContent": "\tpreOutput, err := r.dispatchPreToolUse(ctx, call)",
          "title": "Pre-tool hook dispatch",
          "description": "Allows hooks to inspect, block, or mutate tool input"
        },
        {
          "id": "3c",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 90,
          "lineContent": "\tif preOutput.Decision.IsBlocking() {",
          "title": "Block check",
          "description": "Stops execution if hook returned block decision"
        },
        {
          "id": "3d",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 105,
          "lineContent": "\tresult, execErr := r.executor.Execute(ctx, call.Name, args)",
          "title": "Actual tool execution",
          "description": "Delegates to ToolExecutor (registry adapter) for real execution"
        },
        {
          "id": "3e",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 127,
          "lineContent": "\t_ = r.dispatchPostToolUse(ctx, call, toolResult, durationMS)",
          "title": "Post-tool hook dispatch",
          "description": "Allows hooks to observe result and duration for logging/metrics"
        },
        {
          "id": "3f",
          "path": "internal/engine/tool_runner.go",
          "lineNumber": 129,
          "lineContent": "\treturn toolResult, nil",
          "title": "Result return",
          "description": "Returns tool result to engine for appending to conversation"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "ToolRunner: Hook-Integrated Tool Execution\n├── Execute() entry point \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── Pre-execution phase\n│   │   ├── dispatchPreToolUse() \u003c-- 3b\n│   │   │   └── hooks.Dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:150\n│   │   │       └── Hook skills execute\n│   │   └── Check if blocked \u003c-- 3c\n│   │       └── Return error if blocked \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:92\n│   ├── Core execution phase\n│   │   └── executor.Execute() \u003c-- 3d\n│   │       └── registryToolExecutor.Execute() \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:396\n│   │           └── FuncTool.Call() \u003c-- internal/actor/agent_actor.go:419\n│   └── Post-execution phase\n│       ├── dispatchPostToolUse() \u003c-- 3e\n│       │   └── hooks.Dispatcher.Dispatch() \u003c-- internal/engine/tool_runner.go:185\n│       │       └── Hook skills observe result\n│       └── Return ToolResult \u003c-- 3f\n└── Called by LLMChatEngine.Run() loop \u003c-- internal/engine/llmchat_engine.go:177",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen an LLM agent needs to execute a tool (like reading a file or running a command), the system must provide **observability and control** over these operations. The ToolRunner solves this by wrapping tool execution with **hooks** - allowing external code to inspect, block, or modify tool calls before they run, and observe results after completion [3a]. This enables features like security policies (blocking dangerous operations), logging/metrics, input sanitization, and result transformation without modifying the core tool execution logic.\n\n## Details\n\n### Execution Flow\n\nThe ToolRunner orchestrates a three-phase execution pipeline:\n\n1. **Pre-execution hooks** [3b]: Before the tool runs, `dispatchPreToolUse()` invokes registered hook skills that receive the tool name and input arguments. Hooks can return a `block` decision to prevent execution [3c], or provide `updated_tool_input` to modify the arguments.\n\n2. **Tool execution** [3d]: If not blocked, the ToolRunner delegates to a `ToolExecutor` interface (typically `registryToolExecutor` which adapts the agent's tool registry). The actual tool implementation is called and returns a result string.\n\n3. **Post-execution hooks** [3e]: After execution, `dispatchPostToolUse()` invokes hooks with the tool result and execution duration. This enables logging, metrics collection, and result observation. The final `ToolResult` is returned to the engine [3f].\n\n### Integration\n\nThe ToolRunner is called by `LLMChatEngine` during its tool-calling loop [1d]. Each tool call from the LLM passes through this hook-integrated execution pipeline before the result is appended back to the conversation history. This design keeps the engine focused on LLM interaction while delegating tool execution concerns to a composable, hook-aware layer.\n\n### Hook Contract\n\nHooks are external skills that receive a JSON `Input` with event type, tool metadata, and context. They return an `Output` with a `Decision` (approve/block/none), optional context to inject, and actions to enqueue. The dispatcher merges outputs from multiple hooks using last-wins semantics for mutations."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "DSPyAdapter: Bridge to dspy-go Agent",
      "description": "Agent engine system - adapter implementing AgentEngine interface by wrapping dspy-go ReActAgent",
      "locations": [
        {
          "id": "4a",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 83,
          "lineContent": "func NewDSPyAdapter(cfg DSPyAdapterConfig) (*DSPyAdapter, error) {",
          "title": "Adapter creation",
          "description": "Initializes LLM and creates dspy-go ReActAgent"
        },
        {
          "id": "4b",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 151,
          "lineContent": "\tagent := react.NewReActAgent(a.config.AgentID, a.config.AgentName, opts...)",
          "title": "ReActAgent instantiation",
          "description": "Creates dspy-go agent with max iterations and timeout"
        },
        {
          "id": "4c",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 168,
          "lineContent": "\tif err := agent.Initialize(a.llm, sig); err != nil {",
          "title": "Agent initialization",
          "description": "Initializes agent with LLM and signature (system prompt)"
        },
        {
          "id": "4d",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 197,
          "lineContent": "func (a *DSPyAdapter) Run(ctx context.Context, input EngineInput) (EngineOutput, error) {",
          "title": "AgentEngine.Run implementation",
          "description": "Implements same interface as LLMChatEngine for polymorphism"
        },
        {
          "id": "4e",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 207,
          "lineContent": "\tprompt := a.buildPrompt(input)",
          "title": "Prompt construction",
          "description": "Converts EngineInput messages to single prompt string"
        },
        {
          "id": "4f",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 216,
          "lineContent": "\tresultMap, err := a.reactAgent.Execute(ctx, inputMap)",
          "title": "DSPy execution",
          "description": "Delegates to dspy-go agent which handles its own tool loop"
        },
        {
          "id": "4g",
          "path": "internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go",
          "lineNumber": 235,
          "lineContent": "\tresult := extractResultFromMap(resultMap)",
          "title": "Result extraction",
          "description": "Extracts text response from dspy-go result map"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "DSPyAdapter: AgentEngine Implementation\n├── Adapter Creation \u003c-- 4a\n│   ├── createLLM() - initialize provider \u003c-- internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go:88\n│   └── initializeAgent() \u003c-- internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go:102\n│       ├── NewReActAgent() \u003c-- 4b\n│       └── agent.Initialize(llm, sig) \u003c-- 4c\n│\n└── AgentEngine.Run() interface \u003c-- 4d\n    ├── buildPrompt() - convert messages \u003c-- 4e\n    ├── reactAgent.Execute() \u003c-- 4f\n    │   └── [dspy-go internal tool loop]\n    └── extractResultFromMap() \u003c-- 4g\n        └── return EngineOutput \u003c-- internal/engine/dspy_adapter.go:249",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nfoxctl supports **two different LLM execution engines**: a native `LLMChatEngine` that directly calls OpenAI-compatible APIs, and a legacy path using the `dspy-go` library's ReActAgent. The **DSPyAdapter** exists to make the dspy-go agent conform to the same `AgentEngine` interface [4d] as `LLMChatEngine`, enabling **polymorphic engine selection** at runtime. This allows the `AgentActor` to switch between engines via configuration without changing its message handling logic.\n\nThe problem: dspy-go provides a complete agent implementation with its own tool loop, but it doesn't match foxctl's `AgentEngine` interface. Without an adapter, you'd need duplicate actor code for each engine type.\n\n## Details\n\n**Adapter Creation** happens in two phases [4a]. First, it creates an LLM client based on the provider configuration (Gemini, OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.) [4a]. Then it initializes a dspy-go `ReActAgent` [4b] with max iterations and timeout settings, and calls `Initialize()` with the LLM and a signature (system prompt structure) [4c].\n\n**Execution Flow** starts when `AgentActor` calls `Run()` [4d] with an `EngineInput` containing messages, tools, and system prompt. The adapter converts these messages into a single prompt string [4e] because dspy-go expects a simpler input format. It then delegates to `reactAgent.Execute()` [4f], which runs its own internal tool-calling loop (not visible to foxctl). Finally, it extracts the result string from dspy-go's result map [4g] and wraps it in an `EngineOutput` to return.\n\n**Key Difference**: Unlike `LLMChatEngine` which exposes each tool call iteration, the DSPy adapter treats the entire agent execution as a **black box**—you call `Execute()` once and get back the final result after all tool calls complete internally."
    },
